# {{PROJECT_NAME}} frontend agent guide

This is the React + Vite frontend for the counter contract in the parent
directory. Read the root `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `package.json`, and npm
lockfile when present. Run all commands below from the project root.

## Read the right guidance

Use the Groundhog skill's TypeScript and React references for general APIs.
The project's installed dependencies and existing code take precedence over
examples that use other versions or libraries. Keep the provided client and
wallet helpers unless the task includes changing that architecture.

## Frontend layout

- `main.tsx`: counter UI, reads, and transaction feedback.
- `wallet.ts`: temporary native browser wallet and local funding.
- `generated/counter.ts`: typed contract bindings, regenerated by build/startup.
- `../scripts/groundhog.mjs`: CLI build, binding generation, and deployment.
- `../.groundhog/app.json`: public configuration for the current local deployment.

Regenerate bindings after changing the contract interface. Do not edit generated
bindings or invent selectors, calldata encodings, or deployment addresses.

## Verify changes

- `npm run typecheck`: check TypeScript. Generate bindings first with
  `npm run build` if `web/generated/counter.ts` is missing or stale.
- `npm run build`: rebuild the contract and bindings, check TypeScript, and
  bundle the frontend. This needs Node/npm, the CLI, Rust, and registry access.
- For contract changes, also run the native and RV32 tests from the root guide.

To test the browser flow, follow `README.md`: start `groundhog node`, install
the npm dependencies, and run `npm run dev`. Development startup funds the
configured local wallet, deploys a new counter, and starts Vite. It is not a
read-only check. A working typecheck/build does not verify a live transaction.

## Local account and network behavior

The browser creates a temporary private key in memory and requests local test
funds. A reload creates a new wallet. Preserve this local example's signing
flow; do not substitute a wallet extension or switch to hosted Devnet to follow
a general tutorial. Keep private signing material out of logs and files.

Read the deployment configuration supplied by the dev server. It supplies
`/deployment.json` and proxies `/rpc`; a static bundle needs those services to
work elsewhere. Refresh contract data after confirmed success, and retain the
original transaction hash when its result is uncertain.
